"Takai R","firstName":"Ryosuke","lastName":"Takai","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Molecular Biomedicine for Pathogenesis, Center for Disease Biology and Integrative Medicine, Faculty of Medicine, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo 113-0033, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Yamazaki T","firstName":"Tomoko","lastName":"Yamazaki","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Molecular Biomedicine for Pathogenesis, Center for Disease Biology and Integrative Medicine, Faculty of Medicine, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo 113-0033, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Mori N","firstName":"Nobuko","lastName":"Mori","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Molecular Biomedicine for Pathogenesis, Center for Disease Biology and Integrative Medicine, Faculty of Medicine, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo 113-0033, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Department of Basic Veterinary Medicine, School of Veterinary Medicine, Nippon Veterinary and Life Science University, Tokyo 180-8602, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Okada Y","firstName":"Yuki","lastName":"Okada","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center of Advanced Studies in Veterinary Medicine, School of Veterinary Medicine, Nippon Veterinary and Life Science University, Tokyo 180-8602, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Takeda N","firstName":"Naoki","lastName":"Takeda","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Animal Resources and Development, Kumamoto University, Kumamoto 860-0811, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Yamamura KI","firstName":"Ken-Ichi","lastName":"Yamamura","initials":"KI","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Center for Animal Resources and Development, Kumamoto University, Kumamoto 860-0811, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Arai T","firstName":"Toshiro","lastName":"Arai","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Basic Veterinary Medicine, School of Veterinary Medicine, Nippon Veterinary and Life Science University, Tokyo 180-8602, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Arai S","firstName":"Satoko","lastName":"Arai","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Molecular Biomedicine for Pathogenesis, Center for Disease Biology and Integrative Medicine, Faculty of Medicine, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo 113-0033, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Miyazaki T","firstName":"Toru","lastName":"Miyazaki","initials":"T","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3842-4362"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Molecular Biomedicine for Pathogenesis, Center for Disease Biology and Integrative Medicine, Faculty of Medicine, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo 113-0033, Japan."},{"affiliation":"AMED-CREST, Japan Agency for Medical Research and Development, Tokyo 113-0033, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Max Planck-The University of Tokyo Center for Integrative Inflammology, Tokyo 113-0033, Japan."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5912-5647"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6145-8686"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3842-4362"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data","related_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"6","journalIssueId":2457456,"dateOfPublication":"2016 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2016,"printPublicationDate":"2016-10-01","journal":{"title":"Scientific reports","medlineAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","essn":"2045-2322","issn":"2045-2322","isoabbreviation":"Sci Rep","nlmid":"101563288"}},"pubYear":"2016","pageInfo":"35251","abstractText":"Renal failure is one of the most important social problems for its incurability and high costs for patients' health care. Through clarification of the underlying mechanism for the high susceptibility of cats to renal disease, we here demonstrates that the effective dissociation of serum AIM protein from IgM is necessary for the recovery from acute kidney injury (AKI). In cats, the AIM-IgM binding affinity is 1000-fold higher than that in mice, which is caused by the unique positively-charged amino-acid cluster present in feline AIM. Hence, feline AIM does not dissociate from IgM during AKI, abolishing its translocation into urine. This results in inefficient clearance of lumen-obstructing necrotic cell debris at proximal tubules, thereby impairing AKI recovery. Accordingly, mice whose AIM is replaced by feline AIM exhibit higher mortality by AKI than in wild-type mice. Recombinant AIM administration into the mice improves their renal function and survival. As insufficient recovery from AKI predisposes patients to chronic, end-stage renal disease, feline AIM may be involved crucially in the high mortality of cats due to renal disease. Our findings could be the basis of the development of novel AKI therapies targeting AIM-IgM dissociation, and may support renal function in cats and prolong their lives.","affiliation":"Laboratory of Molecular Biomedicine for Pathogenesis, Center for Disease Biology and Integrative Medicine, Faculty of Medicine, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo 113-0033, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cats"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Kidney Diseases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"VE","qualifierName":"veterinary","majorTopic_YN":"Y"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cat Diseases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology","majorTopic_YN":"Y"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Disease Susceptibili